arXiv:2510.20952v1 [cs.LG] 23 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Models
<br class="ltx_break">for Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sungjun Cho
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Changho Shin
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Suenggwan Jo
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Xinya Yan, Shourjo Aditya Chaudhuri, Frederic Sala</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computer Sciences
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Wisconsin-Madison
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{cho266, cshin23, sjo32, xyan89, sachaudhuri, fsala}@wisc.edu</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Forecasting in the real world requires integrating structured time-series data with unstructured textual information, but existing methods are architecturally limited by fixed input/output horizons and are unable to model or quantify uncertainty. We address this challenge by introducing LLM-integrated Bayesian State space models (LBS), a novel probabilistic framework for multimodal temporal forecasting. At a high level, LBS consists of two components: (1) a state space model (SSM) backbone that captures the temporal dynamics of latent states from which both numerical and textual observations are generated and (2) a pretrained large language model (LLM) that is adapted to encode textual inputs for posterior state estimation and decode textual forecasts consistent with the latent trajectory. This design enables flexible lookback and forecast windows, principled uncertainty quantification, and improved temporal generalization thanks to the well-suited inductive bias of SSMs toward modeling dynamical systems. Experiments on the TextTimeCorpus benchmark demonstrate that LBS improves the previous state-of-the-art by 13.20% while providing human-readable summaries of each forecast. <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Our work is the first to unify LLMs and SSMs for joint numerical and textual prediction, offering a novel foundation for multimodal temporal reasoning</span>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Time-series forecasting, a core machine learning task, is traditionally centered on predicting future numerical values from past data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. However, in many real-world domains, contextual information expressed in natural language—such as clinical notes, financial reports, or weather descriptions—plays a critical role in forecasting. This complementary modality can offer valuable signals that cannot be fully extracted from numeric data alone <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Similarly, generating textual forecasts alongside numerical predictions can improve interpretability. This is particularly useful in high-stakes decision-making scenarios. These opportunities motivate the development of models that not only forecast from multimodal inputs, but also communicate their predictions through natural language, augmenting quantitative accuracy with qualitative explanations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Probabilistic state space models (SSMs) offer compelling advantages for time-series forecasting: they can quantify uncertainty and support flexible, variable-length prediction horizons. For this reason, it is intuitive to seek to integrate a probabilistic SSM with a pretrained large language model (LLM), enabling forecasting from and into both numeric and textual modalities. However, this integration presents two fundamental challenges: <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C1) Text-conditioned posterior state estimation:</span> How can we update the latent state of the SSM using a pretrained LLM and textual observations? <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C2) Latent state-conditioned text generation:</span> How can we adapt the LLM to generate accurate, temporally grounded textual forecasts conditioned on latent state trajectories?</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:182pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-3.3pt,1.8pt) scale(0.981042682406679,0.981042682406679) ;"><img src="2510.20952v1/our_model_1step.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/256;" width="476" height="256" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">An illustration of the LBS architecture under for a single-step forecasting scenario. <span id="S1.F1.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom:</span> To enable variational inference with text, the LLM is tuned to summarize the context into a set of summarization tokens, which are then used together with the target value to obtain the posterior state distribution. <span id="S1.F1.7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top:</span> Conditioned on the state forecast, the shared LLM is trained to generate its corresponding text. When using instruction-tuned LLMs, both steps are accompanied by prompt templates in order to preserve its capabilities.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose the <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLM-integrated Bayesian State Space Model (LBS)</span>, a novel architecture that unifies a probabilistic SSM with a pretrained LLM for joint numeric and textual forecasting (see <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a>). For <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C1)</span>, we adapt the LLM to summarize and compress textual inputs into a sequence of summary tokens, which are then together projected into the low-dimensional latent state space for deep Bayesian filtering. For <span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C2)</span>, we leverage the LLM’s in-context generation capabilities by conditioning it on latent state trajectories—treated as non-textual context, akin to images or videos—enabling the model to generate temporally coherent, state-grounded textual forecasts. To our knowledge, LBS is the first framework to tightly couple probabilistic SSMs and LLMs to generate temporally coherent forecasts across both numeric and textual modalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate LBS on the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TimeTextCorpus (TTC)</span>, a multimodal benchmark spanning climate and clinical domains. Our model consistently outperforms both unimodal and multimodal baselines, <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">improving numeric forecasting accuracy by an average of 13.20%</span> while generating coherent and contextually relevant textual predictions. Qualitative analyses show further benefits of the Bayesian SSM backbone, including its <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">ability to encode trends and seasonality in low-dimensional latent states and provide principled uncertainty estimates</span>. Finally, we explore LLM scaling by substituting various models from the Qwen2.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> and LLaMA3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> families, finding that larger LLMs do not always improve performance, but <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">incorporating textual context enhances robustness especially for long-horizon forecasts</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The key contributions of our work are the following:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:15px;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present LBS, a novel architecture for multimodal time-series forecasting that integrates a Bayesian state space model with pretrained LLMs to enable conditioning posterior estimates on textual inputs as well as text generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We incorporate techniques from the LLM-based context compression and multimodal instruction tuning literature towards enabling LBS to perform posterior state estimation conditioned on text and state-conditioned text generation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We verify the empirical performance of LBS on the multimodal forecasting benchmark, <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TimeTextCorpus (TTC)</span>, achieving excellent performance on climate and medical domains.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly review work related to LBS, specifically those in multimodal time-series forecasting and Bayesian state-space models. An extended discussion on related work can be found in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Extended Related Work ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by recent advancements in LLMs, recent studies have explored their potential to enhance time-series forecasting by incorporating textual context. Some approaches use LLMs directly <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, while others finetune them with auxiliary modules. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> introduce a cross-attention architecture to fuse news and time-series data. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> propose HybridMMF, a joint model trained on the TimeTextCorpus using shared embeddings for text and time series. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> develop ChatTime, a foundation model fine-tuned for diverse zero-shot forecasting tasks. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> present TaTS, which augments standard models with concurrent textual inputs as auxiliary variables.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">These works do not explore the integration of LLMs into state space models, which is the key contribution of our work</span>. By incorporating LLMs into state space models, we not only improve time-series forecasting performance but also exploit the benefits of state space models, such as uncertainty estimation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bayesian State Space Models.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bayesian filtering has long been central to time-series modeling, with the classical Kalman filter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> offering optimal estimation in linear Gaussian systems. To address nonlinear and non-Gaussian dynamics, extensions such as the Extended Kalman Filter, Unscented Kalman Filter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>, Ensemble Kalman Filter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, and Sequential Monte Carlo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> have been proposed to approximate posterior distributions using linearization or sampling techniques.
More recent work combines state space structure with deep neural networks. KVAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> apply neural parameterizations to learn latent dynamics for sequential and multivariate time-series forecasting. In reinforcement learning, models like PlaNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, Dreamer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, and KalmanNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> extend these ideas to partially observable, high-dimensional control tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite this progress, most prior work focuses on unimodal data such as images or numerical sequences. <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Our work is the first to integrate pretrained large language models (LLMs) with a probabilistic state space model for joint numeric and textual forecasting</span>, extending Bayesian state estimation into the realm of multimodal generative modeling.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide background information on multimodal time-series forecasting (§<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Multimodal Forecasting ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>), followed by a discussion on the latent dynamical system we work with (§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Bayesian State Space Models ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>). We also derive the multimodal extension of the evidence lower bound that we use for approximate Bayesian inference. Specific architectural choices made to realize LLM integration are discussed in the following section.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Multimodal Forecasting</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a temporal series of numerical values across <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> time steps <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{1:t}=[{\bm{y}}_{1},\dots,{\bm{y}}_{t}]\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{1:t}=[{\bm{y}}_{1},\dots,{\bm{y}}_{t}]\in\mathbb{R}^{t\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the objective of <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unimodal</span> time-series forecasting is to predict the target values of next <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> steps <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t+1:t+H}=[{\bm{y}}_{t+1},\dots,{\bm{y}}_{t+H}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t+1:t+H}=[{\bm{y}}_{t+1},\dots,{\bm{y}}_{t+H}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the prediction horizon. Depending on the dimension <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> of the prediction target, we refer to the TSF problem as univariate (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1</annotation></semantics></math>) or multivariate (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>). In this work, we will focus on the univariate setup; we note that our method can easily be extended to multivariate scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our <span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">multimodal</span> forecasting setup, we are additionally given a temporally aligned sequence of textual data <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t}=[\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1},\dots,\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t}=[\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1},\dots,\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Each such point is itself a sequence of tokens <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}=[w_{1},\dots,w_{\text{len}(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mtext>len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}=[w_{1},\dots,w_{\text{len}(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})}]</annotation></semantics></math>. Using both target values and textual data, our goal is to train a mapping that predicts future target values as well as corresponding text:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\Theta}:({\bm{y}}_{1:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t})\mapsto({\bm{y}}_{t+1:t+H},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t+1:t+H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\Theta}:({\bm{y}}_{1:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t})\mapsto({\bm{y}}_{t+1:t+H},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t+1:t+H})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that existing multimodal forecasters are commonly trained under rigid assumptions on fixed input windows and prediction horizons with no explicit modeling of uncertainty, learning a deterministic mapping <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f_{\Theta}:({\bm{y}}_{t-L:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t-L:t})\to({\bm{y}}_{t+1:t+H},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t+1:t+H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\Theta}:({\bm{y}}_{t-L:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t-L:t})\to({\bm{y}}_{t+1:t+H},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t+1:t+H})</annotation></semantics></math> with finite lookback size <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and prediction horizon <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. This limitation reduces their adaptability to real-world scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Bayesian State Space Models</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, state space models offer a much flexible alternative to Transformer-architectures, with an inductive bias that better fits the temporal forecasting scenario. For this reason, we adopt a Bayesian state space modeling approach, providing a principled way to capture the underlying latent dynamics governing both numeric and textual observations in an uncertainty-aware manner.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Latent dynamical model.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At each time step <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume that a shared unobservable latent state <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes the system’s internal condition, which evolves stochastically over time and gives rise to both types of data as conditionally independent emissions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure" style="width:44%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.20952v1/graphical_model_pdf.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/121;" width="215" height="121"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The latent dynamical model assumed by LBS. The SSM serves as a backbone modeling the temporal dynamics of latent states <math id="S3.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, from which multimodal data <math id="S3.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are generated.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, the model is defined by three core components (<a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Latent dynamical model. ‣ 3.2 Bayesian State Space Models ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 2</span></a>). First, the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">state transition model</span> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> governs the evolution of latent states over time, modeling the temporal evolution of the system. Second, the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">numeric emission model</span> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{y}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{y}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> captures how the target numeric observations are generated from each latent state. Third, the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">textual emission model</span> <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> models the generation of textual descriptions from the same latent state.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For simplicity, we parameterize the transition distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> as a multivariate Gaussian <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}_{t},\bm{\sigma}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}_{t},\bm{\sigma}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where both the mean <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and diagonal variance <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\sigma}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\sigma}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are produced by a recurrent neural network (e.g., GRU or LSTM) applied to the previous state <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The numeric observation model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{y}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{y}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is also modeled as a Gaussian with fixed variance, the mean of which can be computed by passing <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> through a multi-layer perceptron (MLP). Under this formulation, maximizing the likelihood of the numeric data is equivalent to minimizing the mean squared error (MSE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Modeling the likelihood of text given contextual information—textual or non-textual—is a core strength of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. In our work, we leverage this capability by incorporating a pretrained LLM to model the conditional distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. By finetuning the LLM, we enable it to generate coherent and semantically meaningful text sequences <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> that reflect the underlying dynamics encoded in each latent state <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This design allows LBS to jointly produce interpretable natural language and accurate numerical forecasts, both grounded in a shared latent trajectory.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training objective.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train the model by maximizing the evidence lower bound (ELBO) on the joint likelihood of the observed numeric and textual data (i.e., our loss function is the negative ELBO). The classical ELBO objective naturally extends to our multimodal setting as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_gather ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\log p({\bm{y}}_{1:T},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:T})=\log\int_{{\bm{x}}_{1:T}}\prod_{t=1}^{T}p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t-1})p({\bm{y}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})d{\bm{x}}_{1:T}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mo rspace="0em">∫</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log p({\bm{y}}_{1:T},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:T})=\log\int_{{\bm{x}}_{1:T}}\prod_{t=1}^{T}p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t-1})p({\bm{y}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})d{\bm{x}}_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{E}_{q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})}\bigg[\underbrace{\log p({\bm{y}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})}_{\text{value likelihood}}+\underbrace{\log p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})}_{\text{text likelihood}}\bigg]-\underbrace{\text{KL}(q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{1:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t})\mid\mid p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t-1}))}_{\text{temporal regularization}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≥</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>value likelihood</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>text likelihood</mtext></munder><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mtext>KL</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>temporal regularization</mtext></munder></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{E}_{q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})}\bigg[\underbrace{\log p({\bm{y}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})}_{\text{value likelihood}}+\underbrace{\log p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})}_{\text{text likelihood}}\bigg]-\underbrace{\text{KL}(q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{1:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t})\mid\mid p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t-1}))}_{\text{temporal regularization}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Replacing the computationally intractable posterior distribution <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{1:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{1:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t})</annotation></semantics></math>, we introduce a variational posterior <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{1:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{1:t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{1:t})</annotation></semantics></math> over the latent states, parameterized via a deep Kalman filter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
Intuitively, the training objective effectively balances three essential aspects of Bayesian state estimation. First, the expected likelihood terms ensure fidelity to the observed data by encouraging the latent states to retain enough information to accurately reconstruct both the numeric values and textual descriptions. Second, the KL regularizer imposes temporal coherence by penalizing latent trajectories that deviate too strongly from the prior dynamics controlled by the SSM. Lastly, the variational expectation allows the model to predict under uncertainty in the latent trajectory, inducing more robust and generalizable forecasts.
A full derivation of <a href="#A2.Ex9" title="Appendix B Derivation of Training Objective ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix B</span></a> can be found in the appendix.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_top ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:193.2pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Stateful training step of LBS</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input :</span>  Previous state and hidden <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t-1},{\bm{h}}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t-1},{\bm{h}}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"> Current text and value <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t},{\bm{y}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t},{\bm{y}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output :</span>  Current state and hidden <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t},{\bm{h}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t},{\bm{h}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
              Get prior <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}_{t},\bm{\sigma}_{t}),{\bm{h}}_{t}=\text{SSM}(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t-1},{\bm{h}}_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>SSM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}_{t},\bm{\sigma}_{t}),{\bm{h}}_{t}=\text{SSM}(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t-1},{\bm{h}}_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
              Summarize text <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{s}}_{t}=\text{LLM}_{\text{encoder}}(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>LLM</mtext><mtext>encoder</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{s}}_{t}=\text{LLM}_{\text{encoder}}(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
              Get posterior <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\hat{\bm{\mu}}_{t},\hat{\bm{\sigma}}_{t})=\text{MLP}_{\text{post}}({\bm{h}}_{t},{\bm{y}}_{t},{\bm{s}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>MLP</mtext><mtext>post</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\hat{\bm{\mu}}_{t},\hat{\bm{\sigma}}_{t})=\text{MLP}_{\text{post}}({\bm{h}}_{t},{\bm{y}}_{t},{\bm{s}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
              Sample <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\hat{\bm{\mu}}_{t},\hat{\bm{\sigma}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(\hat{\bm{\mu}}_{t},\hat{\bm{\sigma}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> via reparameterization
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{val}}=\|{\bm{y}}_{t}-\text{MLP}_{\text{val}}(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>val</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>MLP</mtext><mtext>val</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{val}}=\|{\bm{y}}_{t}-\text{MLP}_{\text{val}}(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{text}}=\text{LLM}_{\text{decoder}}(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>LLM</mtext><mtext>decoder</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{text}}=\text{LLM}_{\text{decoder}}(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
              <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{KL}}=\text{KL}(\mathcal{N}(\hat{\bm{\mu}}_{t},\hat{\bm{\sigma}}_{t})\mid\mid\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}_{t},\bm{\sigma}_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mtext>KL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝁</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝝈</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∣</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝈</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{KL}}=\text{KL}(\mathcal{N}(\hat{\bm{\mu}}_{t},\hat{\bm{\sigma}}_{t})\mid\mid\mathcal{N}(\bm{\mu}_{t},\bm{\sigma}_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
              Update parameters via <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{L}}_{\text{val}}+{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{text}}+{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>val</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>text</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>KL</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}={\mathcal{L}}_{\text{val}}+{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{text}}+{\mathcal{L}}_{\text{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">9</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm1.4.12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t},{\bm{h}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒉</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t},{\bm{h}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>


</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:138.0pt;">
          
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.20952v1/singlestep_pipeline.svg" id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:191/190;" width="191" height="190"></object>
</div>
          
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of a single forward pass through LBS.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
        
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>LBS: LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Model</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To integrate textual information into our latent dynamical model, we must address two technical challenges: <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C1) How can we design the LLM-based filter that estimates the a posteriori state conditioned on text (i.e., <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>)?</span> <span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(C2) How can we model the likelihood of text conditioned on the latent state (i.e. <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid{\bm{x}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>)?</span> In this section, we detail the architectural components that tackle these two challenges. The components form the foundation of our proposed framework LBS. An illustration of the architecture can be found in <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Text-conditioned Posterior State Estimation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To efficiently update our prior state estimates conditioned on text, we draw inspiration from recent work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and adapt the pretrained LLM for context compression towards fast one-shot encoding of textual observations into latent state summaries.
Then, we forward latent state summaries together with the prior state <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and numeric value <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> for posterior inference.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Text Compression.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The core idea is to introduce special tokens unique to the task of summarization, and finetune the LLM to store critical information in the summary tokens for effective posterior inference. Concretely, we first augment the vocabulary of the pretrained LLM with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> special learnable tokens <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{&lt;SUM&gt;}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;SUM&gt;</mtext><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{&lt;SUM&gt;}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which facilitate the task of text compression. To encode a textual observation <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we append all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> summary tokens after the input sequence <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mathcal{D}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mathcal{D}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and forward the augmented sequence through the pretrained LLM. While also possible to randomize the positions of summary tokens during training to mitigate positional biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, we empirically found that it leads to negligible changes in performance, possibly due to lack of variance in text length.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">After processing the sequence through the LLM, we extract the final hidden states of the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> inserted summary tokens, then concatenate along the feature dimension to form a single summary vector of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This vector is then projected through a MLP to obtain a low-dimensional summary vector <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{s}}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{s}}_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> that matches the latent states in dimension.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Posterior Inference.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the summary vector <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{s}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{s}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the mean and diagonal covariance of the variational posterior distribution <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{t},{\bm{s}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{t},{\bm{s}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, assumed to be Gaussian, via a neural Kalman filter parameterized by another MLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. This MLP takes as input the summary vector <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{s}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{s}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the corresponding numeric target <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the prior latent state <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and outputs the mean and log-variance of the posterior distribution. Note the entire process is end-to-end trainable, hence we finetune the LLM using LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>)</cite> to effectively encode forecasting-relevant semantic and temporal information into the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{&lt;SUM&gt;}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">&lt;SUM&gt;</mtext><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{&lt;SUM&gt;}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> tokens without significantly altering the generative capabilities within its pretrained weights. The detailed prompt used to compress text can be fo und in the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>State-conditioned Text Generation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given the posterior distribution, we use the reparameterization trick <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> to generate <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t}\sim q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t}\sim q({\bm{x}}_{t}\mid{\bm{y}}_{t},\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, generating Monte-Carlo samples in an end-to-end learnable manner.
Then, we can model the posterior state-conditioned textual likelihood <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}\mid\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> by providing a projection of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as a prefix to the LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, similarly to vision-text instruction tuning frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.
Specifically, we project the sampled low-dimensional latent state vector <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{x}}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> into a sequence of tokens for the LLM using a linear layer. These projected tokens are then prepended to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively allowing the LLM to condition its generation on the state dynamics captured by the temporal SSM backbone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This design assumes that the temporal backbone is capable of encoding time-specific information such as event structure, trends, or contextual shifts within a compact latent space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. By projecting this information into the high-dimensional language space and augmenting it with instruction prompts, we provide the LLM with the necessary information to generate fluent and temporally consistent text.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Shared or Separate LLMs?</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While it is possible to use two separate LLMs for encoding and decoding, for LBS we assume the same LLM weights are shared between the two steps: the LLM that encodes text into compressed embeddings for posterior estimation also serves as the decoder for text generation. This weight sharing not only reduces the computational burden, but also encourages the LLM to encode forecasting-relevant information in a way that it can later reuse for generation, learning prediction and inference in a self-consistent manner.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Stateful single-step training.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Ideally, training LBS on long sequences would allow the model to better capture long-range dependencies. However, each timestep <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> requires passing <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝓓</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{{\mathcal{D}}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> through the LLM twice—once for encoding and once for decoding—making naïve long-horizon training computationally expensive. To address this, we adopt <span id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">stateful</span> training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, where the model is trained on single-step batches under its temporal ordering, with hidden states passed onto the next training iteration. The detailed algorithm and illustration of a single training step can be found in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Training objective. ‣ 3.2 Bayesian State Space Models ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Training objective. ‣ 3.2 Bayesian State Space Models ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experimental Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically explore the following claims about LBS.</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:15px;">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 1: LBS improves performance in multimodal time-series forecasting. (§5.1)</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 2: LBS can model predictive uncertainty using its probabilistic architecture. (§5.2)</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 3: LBS encodes trends and seasonality within its latent states. (§5.3)</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Claim 4: Scaling the LLM in LBS improves performance. (§5.4)</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.20952v1/ttc_main_results_pdf.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/174;" width="476" height="174"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Test RMSE results from <span id="S5.F4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TTC-Climate</span> (left) and <span id="S5.F4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TTC-Medical</span> (right). </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We perform experiments on the <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TextTimeCorpus</span> (<span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TTC</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>), a multimodal time-series forecasting benchmark that covers two distinct domains: <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TTC-Climate</span> consists of daily temperature measurements at Washington DC with textual weather descriptions. <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TTC-Medical</span> consists of daily heart rate measurements from hospitalized patients accompanied by nursing notes. Following previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, we use a 8-1-1 train-validation-test split across time. Further details on the datasets can be found in the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Time-Series Forecasting Performance</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first compare our model against existing multimodal forecasters TSFLib <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, TextTime2TextTime (TT2TT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>), and HybridMMF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. For TSFLib, we use Reformer as its time-series forecasting backbone, as it was the best-performing setup. We also compare against unimodal methods, results from which we defer to <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Additional Experiment Details ‣ LLM-Integrated Bayesian State Space Modelsfor Multimodal Time-Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix C</span></a> for brevity. Note that all baselines except LBS are specifically trained for each prediction horizon <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, a single LBS model is evaluated on all possible <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> to demonstrate its generalizability to arbitrary forecast horizons. All multimodal models adopt LLaMA3.1-8B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> as the base LLM, and LBS uses a single-layer GRU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> with latent dimension 16 as the SSM backbone. Further details on training and model hyperparameters can be found in the appendix.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table" style="width:50%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Test RMSE and relative improvements from incorporating textual data in LBS, averaged across all prediction horizons.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T1.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:172.5pt;height:40.3pt;vertical-align:-18.1pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-19.9pt,4.7pt) scale(0.812188167673302,0.812188167673302) ;">
<table id="S5.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt">Method</th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TTC-Climate</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.5.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.5.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">TTC-Medical</span></th></tr>
</thead>
